cept this um and be humble about  understanding what we're doing wrong and  improving for the future and this is  what we're trying to do we're trying to  address this problem and we're trying to  do the best that we can so that this  doesn't happen again  lessons learned thank you  how do you deal with the mix of pro and  active actual volunteers how do you make  decisions when some are working and  others are volunteering  yeah i think this is a very  important question and something that we  try to do  um with numpy and scipy is  having a strong governance model that  includes people both from professional  and volunteer points of view and that we  enable everyone to have a voice and so  my own particular understanding is that  people who are professionally working in  open source should make work easier for  the volunteers and so that's the basics  that we we stand on which is  doing the heavy lifting doing the you  know boring work  ci taking care of things that volunteers  don't usually pick up and so that's how  we started working on the documentation  and and creating the documentation team  because a lot of people they want to do  new features they want to work on code  and they want to start contributing to  get their names um  you know as contributors to numpy and  usually they don't want to do the heavy  lifting of reorganizing documentation  and things like that and so  if you do have the ability to have  professionals uh working on your open  source project and you can have money  for that and you can have  people who are paid to do that  pick up the things that are not usually  done by volunteers that's where you  should be spending your money on so that  you can have a well-rounded project  supported by all sides  that's what i believe in i do think it's  a delicate problem and that we need to  put that explicit and governance which  is not to  let  companies  take over the project or decide  everything for the project if you want  to have volunteer contributions you need  to include them in decision making as  well  yeah thank you with three more questions  how can we encourage women and mothers  especially to contribute more  this is an um interesting question for  me i'm a mom as well um i do think that  this is  hard but we need to  make sure that we have  the support that people need and this is  not only for mothers there are people  who take care of their  relatives they have elderly people that  they need to take care of and there's  all sorts of situations that  could could be looked at  kind of the same way  i think that giving people expectation  setting  expectations so that they know how they  can contribute how much time they'll  spend in each task how much time are  they expected to spend every week so  that they can plan ahead  if you're doing uh events if you're  doing in-person meetups that you have a  space for children or that you have a  way for people to  care for children or  i don't know that you have child care a  lot of meetups and groups are already  doing that i think it's super important  but more than that i think it's enabling  people to do work on their own rhythm  and so not expecting that they'll answer  right away or  enabling them to do smaller tasks at a  time so that they can pick up this pro  these projects and plan ahead with the  time that they have  um  at least that's what i see from my  experience my biggest difficulty being a  mother is finding the free time usually  when you have any free time you're  spending with your family and so being  able to pick up one hour here one hour  there  is just  amazing and that's when you can do  actual work if you have to spend five  hours reading the manual to be able to  start then that's harder and so  having these smaller tasks and enabling  people to have different entry points  into the project  might help  thank you  for maintainers managing the project is  a huge task how can they focus on  diversity alongside without burning out  yes so i do think that this is something  that asks a lot of the maintainers and  which is why  it  usually  we have maintainers doing everything in  a project it's very hard to have  specialization so  the same people who are reviewing code  they're writing documentation and  organizing community are doing all of  this kind of work together um  one possibility is to have people who  are focused exclusively on community and  improving diversity or contributor  experience and doing that  focusing on that so they don't have to  split themselves between several tasks  and i know this is not easy for smaller  projects  so what i would say is do what you can  you don't have to do everything at once  because i do understand that this is a  big ask and i do understand that this is  hard on  especially volunteer maintainers  especially if you have a an open source  project that is not professionally  maintained and so do what you can start  small start with what you can do  um  but yeah consider doing separating these  tasks or at least writing them up for  one example is that we don't usually  put community uh tasks as issues but  maybe you could maybe you could be very  explicit about what you want to achieve  in terms of community and other people  can come and help you with that it  doesn't have to be one person doing  everything  but for that you need to be able to  delegate you need to be able to organize  these tasks and what you see for the  vision for your project  so that's what i would recommend for  smaller projects and i would love to  hear from you as well if you have  suggestions if you have ideas if you  have experience with that i would love  to hear  thank you last question  what do conferences add to or the open  source ecosystem  where are conferences good what could  conference organizers do better in the  future  well one thing that i noted and that i i  also know it's a big ask i know it's  hard and you folks must be experiencing  this right 